# Insert texts for revise_round5_a.py, part 2 (Sections 3.4-3.9, evidence qualifier, Appendices D-E)
INS = {}

INS['c3_a'] = r"""\citet[pp.~33--35]{garicano2026comment} uses a knowledge-hierarchy model in which humans with knowledge $z$ and AI with knowledge $z_{AI}$ solve problems, compute capacity is $\mu$, and the stock of addressable opportunities becomes the bottleneck when compute is abundant. Who is paid depends on what is scarce. With autonomous AI and a compute glut, humans with less knowledge than AI become unemployed and rents flow to the owners of opportunities; with non-autonomous AI, every problem needs a person, human time is the bottleneck, and wages compress rather than collapse (p.~34). He then endogenises opportunities, which entrepreneurs create at unit cost $C$, and shows that with autonomous AI, entry continues while $C<z_{AI}$, keeping compute scarce and wages positive (p.~35). We keep the problem-solving primitives, the autonomy distinction, free entry and zero profit. We change four things. We drop the escalation layer, so each holder handles one opportunity and, working with AI, solves $s_j=z_A+(1-z_A)z_j$.\footnote{In Garicano's environment a worker who fails can escalate to a more knowledgeable solver, at a cost in the solver's time (p.~33). Dropping this layer removes the solver wage schedule, which the comparisons in Proposition~\ref{prop:autonomy} do not need.} We consider only the case $\mu>H$, in which compute is slack under non-autonomy. We read non-autonomy through our premise: an AI that cannot supply the conversion input needs a person in each slot. And we let $C$ depend on the density $D$ of the local conversion layer. The work that $C$ stands for, ``specifying the job, providing the right data, verifying the result, and making it safe to pay for'' (p.~35), is largely conversion work. Garicano's environment assumes that AI can acquire tacit knowledge (p.~33). Ours does not, and we borrow only the structure.

"""

INS['c3_post'] = r"""
\paragraph{The key step.} An opportunity is worth one if solved, and a holder solves it with probability $s_j$. With free entry and competition for each holder, zero profit holds opportunity by opportunity:
\[
s_j\;=\;C(D)\;+\;r\;+\;w_j .
\]
The expected value of a solved opportunity pays for three things: its packaging, the compute it uses, and the holder. Which of the last two receives the residual depends on what is scarce. Under non-autonomy, every opportunity needs a holder and there is more compute than holders, so compute is free, $r^*=0$, and the holder receives the whole residual $s_j-C(D)$. Under autonomy, an opportunity can also be run by AI alone, earning $z_A-C-r$. Entry into such opportunities continues until compute is fully used and priced at $r^*=z_A-C(D)$. A holder is then paid only for what she adds to AI, $s_j-C-r^*=(1-z_A)z_j$.

\paragraph{Intuition.} Under non-autonomy the holder is the bottleneck, so every saving in packaging cost reaches her, and so does every improvement in what AI solves, unless she already solves every problem ($z_j=1$). A rise in $z_A$ helps low-knowledge holders most, because it fills in the problems they would otherwise fail. A fall in $C$ is the same absolute gain for every employed holder, which is why wage ratios compress. Under autonomy the bottleneck moves to compute. A fall in $C$ raises the rental of compute one for one and leaves wages unchanged. A rise in $z_A$ raises what AI earns alone and lowers what the holder adds to it.

\paragraph{Comparative statics.} Under non-autonomy, the wage gain from a denser conversion layer, $c_1/D^2$ per unit of $D$, is larger where the layer is thin (low $D$) and where packaging depends heavily on it (high $c_1$). Employment expands, because more holders clear $s_j\ge C(D)$. The gain from a rise in $z_A$ is $1-z_j$ per unit, largest for low-knowledge holders. Under autonomy, $D$ and $c_1$ do not affect wages, and a rise in $z_A$ lowers them by $z_j$ per unit. The condition $C(D)<z_A$ under which part~(ii) applies is easier to meet the denser the layer.

\paragraph{What the proposition does not say.} It does not say that autonomy lowers output or total welfare: in part~(ii) the gains from cheaper packaging go to the owners of compute. It does not cover autonomy with $C(D)\ge z_A$. In Garicano's analysis that case stalls the creation of opportunities and lets their price absorb the value (p.~35), and it lies outside part~(ii). It does not model how the conversion layer is built or what it costs, and it treats $D$ as given (Section~\ref{sec:synthesis}). It says nothing about holders with $s_j<C(D)$ except that they are not employed.

\paragraph{Magnitudes.} In the calibration, raising $D$ from 1 to 4 lowers $C$ from 0.35 to 0.125, a wage gain of 0.225 for every employed holder, and lowers the wage ratio between holders with $z=0.8$ and $z=0.2$ from 1.73 to 1.43 (Section~\ref{sec:quant}). For comparison, \citet[p.~24]{kogan2023displacement} find that even labour-augmenting exposure lowered incumbents' cumulative five-year earnings by 1--1.7\% (median to 90th percentile), while entrants gained. That is evidence on who gains from new tasks, not a measurement of $C$.

\paragraph{How AI and institutions move these parameters.} Garicano's example of non-autonomy is radiology. Models beat radiologists on some benchmarks, yet in practice the systems remain non-autonomous, because real-world variation and liability keep a person in each slot (p.~34). Non-autonomy is thus partly a matter of rules. \citet[\S12.6.3, p.~318]{brynjolfsson2026hayek} note that sectoral laws in health and other fields may continue to require human oversight, and that professional licensing imposes human-in-the-loop obligations. On the packaging side, Garicano names data access, verification, liability and payment systems as what shapes $C$ (p.~35); payment codes that allow intermediated services to be billed are one instance (Section~\ref{sec:implications}). We read no measurement of $C$, or of how it responds to the density of intermediaries.

\paragraph{The clinic (illustration).} An opportunity is a follow-up that someone can be paid for. Turning a patient's vague need, such as ``she has not been back since her last reading'', into such a task means knowing whom to contact, what to ask, what to check, and how the visit will be paid for; that is $C$. Where the clinic has many workers who know their patients, $C$ is low and more follow-ups are worth doing. Under a non-autonomous assistant each follow-up needs a worker, so the workers gain. If the assistant could contact patients and adjust treatment on its own, part~(ii) would apply, and cheaper packaging would raise the return to compute, not workers' pay. The example says nothing about whether any current assistant is autonomous in this sense.
"""

INS['c4_a'] = r"""\citet{athey2026competition} study a foreign AI monopolist that sells to firms in a two-sector general-equilibrium model, with a per-unit usage fee and a per-firm access fee. Market power can blunt pass-through, leak rents abroad and harm unskilled workers (pp.~99, 106). They contrast their setting with the standard nonlinear-pricing problem of industrial organisation. There, with a fixed number of firms and no general-equilibrium effects, access fees do not distort production while usage fees do, so a monopolist keeps usage fees low and extracts surplus through access fees. In their model both fees change the size of the surplus through their effect on income (p.~107). We keep the upstream supplier, the two-part tariff and leakage, and we do not rebuild their general equilibrium. The proposition below is partial equilibrium. It takes the intermediated demand of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d) and asks how pass-through, and an access fee charged to intermediaries, change the progressivity threshold.

"""

INS['c4_post'] = r"""
\paragraph{The key step.} Two standard tools do the work \citep{weyl2013passthrough}. By pass-through, a cost decline $dk<0$ lowers the supplier's price by $\rho|dk|$, so households, who buy $Q$, gain $\rho Q|dk|$ to first order. By the envelope theorem, the supplier's profit rises by $Q|dk|$: at the optimum a small price change has no first-order effect on profit, so only the direct cost saving on current output counts. Domestic welfare adds the households' gain to the domestic share $1-f$ of the supplier's gain. For the distributional condition, the access fee is the key term. It is a fixed amount per intermediary, and intermediaries price at zero profit, so it is spread over clients, $q_A=m+(F+A)/n$. It therefore enters the effective price at the bottom of the distribution exactly as the intermediary's own fixed cost does, and it raises the density threshold to $n^*(A)$.

\paragraph{Comparison with the textbook result.} In the textbook nonlinear-pricing problem an access fee does not distort production. In \citet[p.~107]{athey2026competition} it does, because it changes income and entry. In our setting it is not neutral either, for a third reason. The intermediary passes the fee to its clients, and a fee per intermediary costs more per client where there are fewer clients. An access fee therefore bears hardest on low-density catchments, which are the first to lose the guarantee of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d). The two models agree that both fees matter. They differ in the mechanism, and ours does not model the entry of intermediaries.

\paragraph{Intuition and comparative statics.} A supplier with market power keeps part of every cost decline. Households receive the fraction $\rho$, and the progressivity condition is relaxed by the same $\rho|dk|$, so with lower pass-through a given fall in cost moves the economy less far toward inclusiveness. Foreign ownership $f$ lowers the domestic welfare gain from a cost decline but does not enter (d2$_A$): in this model leakage is a level effect, not a distributional one. The threshold $n^*(A)$ rises linearly in $A$, with slope $1/[(1-\eta)R(y_L)-p-m-e(1-v_I)L]$. The slope is steeper where the margin between willingness to pay at the bottom and the other components of the effective price is thin.

\paragraph{What the proposition does not say.} It does not say what $\rho$ is for AI services. Under log-concave demand it lies in $(0,1]$, and the model does not pin it down further. It does not say that a supplier who raises the access fee makes households worse off overall: if the supplier lowers the usage price at the same time, the net effect on $n^*$ is ambiguous, as the proposition states. It does not model entry or exit of intermediaries, so $n$ is given. And it is partial equilibrium: the general-equilibrium effects on wages that \citet{athey2026competition} study are absent.

\paragraph{Magnitudes.} In the calibration without an access fee, $n^*=49$. The threshold rises by about ten clients per unit of access fee, the slope $n^*/F$ with $F=5$. In the weak-necessity construction of Table~\ref{tab:necessity}, an access fee of $A=30$ raises $n^*$ to 345, above the $n=200$ clients per intermediary, and relative gains then rise with income on 5.4\% of the steps of the income grid.

\paragraph{How AI moves these parameters.} \citet[pp.~110--111]{athey2026competition} point to forces on both sides. The AI stack spans chips, training, models and applications, and a single bottleneck can lead to high downstream prices, while open models, competitive entrants or fast followers may discipline incumbents (p.~110). They note that the arrival of DeepSeek showed how hard it may be to keep countries from fast-following others' innovations (p.~111). They also warn of capability loss: adoption that displaces domestic production that is costly to rebuild weakens a country's outside option in bargaining over AI prices (p.~111). We read no estimate of the pass-through of AI cost declines to end users in low-income settings, or of the access fees that intermediaries face (Section~\ref{sec:evidence}). Of all the model's parameters, $\rho$ and $A$ are the least measured. Which layer of the stack is the bottleneck, and how its pricing reaches the fees paid by clinics and other intermediaries, is an open question that \citet[p.~110]{athey2026competition} also raise.

\paragraph{The clinic (illustration).} The supplier charges the clinic a usage fee per consultation, part of $p$, and may charge an access fee per clinic, $A$. A clinic in a dense town spreads $A$ over many patients. A rural clinic with few patients per worker cannot, and where $n<n^*(A)$ the guarantee of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d) no longer holds for its patients. A cap on the usage fee alone can shift extraction to the access fee \citep[p.~110]{athey2026competition}; in this model that shift falls hardest on the thinnest catchments.
"""

INS['c5_a'] = r"""Three known arguments meet here. \citet{becker1962investment} shows that firms in competitive labour markets do not pay for general training, because workers can take it elsewhere; workers pay for it through lower wages. \citet{arrow1962learning} treats knowledge as acquired in the course of production. \citet{ide2025automation} combines the two ideas in a task-based overlapping-generations model in which novices learn by working beside experts and cannot pledge their future earnings, because tacit knowledge is neither verifiable nor usable as collateral (pp.~26--27). We keep Ide's friction. With it, Becker's resolution fails, because novices cannot pay for training through a wage below zero. We change the margin. Ide's mechanism works through which mentors novices are matched with. Ours works through how many novices are apprenticed at all, and therefore through how many mentors the next cohort has.

"""

INS['c5_post6'] = r"""
\paragraph{The key step.} A firm compares two ways of getting an entry task done. A novice costs the supervision cost $\kappa_j$ and a wage of zero; AI costs $p_c$. The firm takes the novice if $\kappa_j\le p_c$. Society counts one more benefit, the discounted premium $\beta W(p_c)$ that the novice will earn as an expert, so the efficient rule is $\kappa_j\le p_c+\beta W(p_c)$. The gap between the two rules is the external value $\beta W$. It cannot be closed by contract, because the novice cannot pay it upfront, and the firm does not capture it, because the knowledge is portable. Under a uniform $G$, the omitted apprenticeships form a band of width $\beta W$ in supervision cost, and the welfare loss is the triangle $(\beta W)^2/(2\bar\kappa)$.

\paragraph{Intuition: the scissors.} AI moves the two rules in opposite directions. It lowers $p_c$, the private value of a novice to the firm, so fewer apprentices are hired. Through Proposition~\ref{prop:valueshift} it raises $W$, the value of what an apprentice learns, so each omitted apprenticeship is worth more to society. One blade lowers the market rate; the other raises the external value $\beta W$ of each omitted apprenticeship. With a uniform $G$ and $p_c+\beta W\le\bar\kappa$, the gap between the market and efficient rates then widens (Proposition~\ref{prop:apprentice}); for other distributions of supervision costs the model does not sign the change in the gap.
"""

INS['c5_postcor'] = r"""
\paragraph{Intuition for the corollary.} An apprentice not trained today is a mentor missing tomorrow. If each expert can train at most $\chi$ apprentices, one cohort's shortfall caps the next cohort's training. With $\chi$ well above one the cap is soon relaxed. With $\chi$ close to one it binds for several cohorts, and the cumulative shortfall exceeds the one-cohort fall whenever $\chi a_L<a_H$ (part~(iii)). With $\chi=1$ the cap never relaxes, and with $\chi<1$ it tightens every period.
"""

INS['c5_post'] = r"""
\paragraph{Comparative statics.} Under a uniform $G$, the gap $a^*-a^m=\beta W/\bar\kappa$ and the loss are larger the more weight is placed on the next period (higher $\beta$), the larger the expert premium $W$, and the denser the distribution of supervision costs (smaller $\bar\kappa$, so that $g=1/\bar\kappa$ is larger). The subsidy that restores the optimum, $s^*=\beta W$, rises as AI cheapens entry tasks. So does the subsidy that keeps the cohort share at its pre-AI level, $\bar p-p_c$. When capacity binds, recovery after a temporary fall takes $\lceil\ln(a_H/a_L)/\ln\chi\rceil$ cohorts, longer the closer $\chi$ is to one. With $\chi=1$ there is no recovery, and with $\chi<1$ the share declines whatever demand does.

\paragraph{What the results do not say.} They do not say that AI must reduce the stock of tacit knowledge. The subsidy results show that the fall can be offset, and AI can also be used for practice rather than substitution, which the model does not represent. They do not say how $t$ is distributed across incomes within the next cohort, which is not modelled (Section~\ref{sec:synthesis}). They take $W$ as independent of the stock of experts; if $W$ fell with the stock, the efficient rate would solve a fixed point. And the subsidy is lump-sum financed, so who pays for it is outside the model.

\paragraph{Magnitudes.} In the calibration of Section~\ref{sec:quant}, market apprenticeship falls by 16\% to 0.672, against an efficient rate of 0.763, and the four values of $\chi$ in Figure~\ref{fig:capacity} cover the four cases of Corollary~\ref{cor:capacity}, from recovery within one cohort ($\chi=1.2$) to geometric decline ($\chi=0.95$). In the data, employment of 22--25-year-olds in the most AI-exposed occupations stands 19\% below a kept-pace counterfactual \citep[August 2026 version, p.~2]{brynjolfsson2025canaries}, and junior employment at firms adopting generative AI falls by roughly 9\% relative to senior employment after six quarters \citep[June 2026 version, p.~5]{hosseini2025seniority}. These measure entry hiring, not apprenticeship.

\paragraph{How AI moves these parameters.} AI acts on demand for apprentices, $a$, and possibly on mentor capacity, $\chi$. The entry-hiring declines just cited are evidence on $a$. On the other side, in the call-centre deployment of \citet{brynjolfsson2025work}, agents with less than a month of tenure gained 46\% in resolutions per hour (WP p.~16, Fig.~5B), and gains persisted during software outages, which the authors read as learning (p.~3); agents also frequently ignored the recommendations (p.~18). In the Penda deployment, the share of AI-group visits in which the first AI output was red fell from 45\% to 35\% over the study, which the authors read as clinicians learning \citep[p.~18]{korom2025penda}. Tools of this kind may raise what a novice learns per period, or let an expert supervise more novices, a higher $\chi$; the studies measure neither. \citet[p.~277, fn.~6]{ludwig2026reflective} frame the design choice as a trade-off between cognitive economy and the benefits of agency.

\paragraph{The clinic (illustration).} A new community health worker learns her district by doing routine calls and home visits beside an experienced worker. If the assistant does the routine calls, the clinic needs fewer trainees: the private value of a trainee, $p_c$ in the model, falls. What the trainee would have learned, such as how to read a household or which patients need a visit rather than a call, is worth more once the assistant has made routine cognition cheap. That is the scissors. If each experienced worker can supervise only one or two trainees, a year in which the clinic hires no trainees leaves it with fewer supervisors later, and Corollary~\ref{cor:capacity} applies. The model does not say whether such an assistant would in practice teach trainees or replace their practice.
"""

INS['liab_a'] = r"""The question is who bears the risk of AI error. The standard result in insurance economics is that a risk-averse household offered insurance at an actuarially fair premium prefers to insure fully. When the choice is between full pooling and none and the premium carries a loading, the household pools only if its risk premium exceeds the loading cost. Proposition~\ref{prop:liability} applies this result to the allocation of AI-error risk among users, intermediaries and suppliers, with two additions: risk aversion falls with income, and the error probability may depend on verification capacity.

"""

INS['liab_post'] = r"""
\paragraph{The key step.} The risk premium is the gap between the certainty-equivalent cost of the loss lottery and its expected value. For small risks it is approximately
\[
RP(y)\;\approx\;\tfrac{1}{2}\,\lambda(y)\,\pi(1-\pi)\,L^2 ,
\]
which is the Arrow--Pratt approximation \citep{pratt1964risk}: half the coefficient of absolute risk aversion times the variance of the loss.\footnote{This is the second-order expansion of $RP$ in $L$ around zero. The proposition uses the exact expression.} Two features follow. The premium scales with $\lambda(y)$, which falls with income, so the same exposure costs a poorer household more. And it scales with $L^2$, so large losses matter disproportionately.

\paragraph{Intuition.} A household that bears its own error risk pays twice for an error it may act upon: once in the expected loss, and again as a premium for bearing the uncertainty. The second payment is larger for poorer households, because a given loss hurts them more. Pooling removes the second payment and keeps the first. Because the pooled premium is experience-rated, households that verify less still pay more, so pooling does not undo the verification channel.

\paragraph{Comparative statics.} The risk premium rises with risk aversion $\lambda$ (proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:liability}) and, for small risks, with the loss $L$ (the approximation above); for given $(\pi,L)$ it falls with income. The set of households that prefer pooling is larger the smaller the loading $\ell$. With $\pi$ common, it is an interval at the bottom of the distribution whose upper end rises as $\ell$ falls. With $\ell=0$ every household weakly prefers pooling.

\paragraph{What the proposition does not say.} It does not say that pooling makes AI use progressive. Pooling removes one regressive term and leaves the verification gradient (part~(iii)). The model has no moral hazard, which is why we do not assume community rating. And it does not say who should hold the pooled risk (user, intermediary or supplier), only that pooling at experience-rated premiums removes the risk premium wherever the risk is held.

\paragraph{Magnitudes.} In the calibration, with $\lambda(y)=1/y$, the average risk premium is 1.94 in the bottom income decile and 0.06 in the top, in units of the median household's willingness to pay (Section~\ref{sec:quant}). The posted price of the AI service is 0.05 in the same units.

\paragraph{How rules move this parameter.} AI does not change $\lambda$; rules change who bears $L$. Directive (EU) 2024/2853 brings software within the definition of a product (Art.~4) and introduces presumptions of defectiveness and causation (Art.~10) \citep{eu2024pld}. The Swedish Patient Injury Act requires care providers to hold patient insurance (§12) and compensates injury that, with a preponderance of probability, was caused by avoidable care (§6) \citep{sweden1996patient}. In the model's terms, such rules move part of $L$ from the household to the producer or to an insurance pool; how much depends on enforcement, which the model does not represent. \citet[p.~318]{brynjolfsson2026hayek} note that liability and insurance rules for AI are yet to be written.

\paragraph{The clinic (illustration).} If a patient follows wrong advice and is harmed, someone bears the loss: the patient, the worker or the clinic, or the supplier. A poor patient who bears it alone pays the risk premium of part~(i) on top of the expected loss. Pooling the risk, through a no-fault scheme of the Swedish kind or insurance held by the clinic, removes that premium. Part~(iii) adds that if patients' premiums were rated on their own error exposure, those who verify less would still pay more, so pooling does not substitute for review. In the Penda design, all final decisions were made by clinicians \citep[p.~7]{korom2025penda}, which places the decision, though not necessarily the loss, with the clinician.
"""

INS['synth_post'] = r"""\paragraph{How the conditions depend on one another.} The proof shows three links. First, the verification guarantee is split between conditions~2 and~4. Condition~2 supplies routing (d1) and the residual gradient condition (d3); the density condition (d2) of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d), once access fees are included, is condition~4's inequality $n\ge n^*(A)$. The supplier's fee structure and the size of the intermediary layer therefore jointly decide whether the guarantee holds. Second, conditions~1 and~3 multiply. The factor-income part of $\Gamma(y)/y$ is the product of the per-unit gain $g(y)$ and holdings relative to income, $\tau(y)/y$. Condition~3 makes $g$ non-negative and non-increasing, and condition~1 makes $\tau(y)/y$ non-increasing; when either fails, the product can increase with income even though the other holds, as the C1 and C3 rows of Table~\ref{tab:necessity} show. Third, condition~5 is the only one that looks forward. It guarantees the next cohort's share of holders, not its distribution across incomes, and in the model it does not feed back into the current cohort's gains. The liability condition enters only through the consumer part, by removing the risk premium and rescaling $L$.

\paragraph{Which conditions are closest to failing.} The model does not rank the conditions by how likely they are to fail; that is an empirical question. It does show which of them hold with little room in the calibration. Condition~5 holds with equality: the subsidy is set at $s=\bar p-p_c=0.128$, so a further fall in $p_c$ without a higher subsidy would violate it and lower $T_1$ below $T_0$. The density condition holds with room to spare, $n=200$ against $n^*=49$, but an access fee of the size used in Table~\ref{tab:necessity} removes that room. Default routing, one item of condition~2, did not matter in the calibration, because the households that under-demand intermediation are near the top of the distribution.

\paragraph{The clinic, put together (illustration).} The clinic's AI assistant spreads gains toward poorer patients and workers, in the model, when the following hold. The workers keep the knowledge of their patients (condition~1). Advice reaches patients through a worker and a reviewing doctor by default, the clinic serves enough patients to spread the cost of review, and losses from errors are pooled (condition~2 and liability). The assistant needs a worker for each follow-up, follow-ups are cheap to organise, and the knowledge of workers does not fall with their household income (condition~3). The supplier's fees leave the clinic above the density threshold (condition~4). And new workers are trained beside experienced ones in sufficient numbers (condition~5). Proposition~\ref{prop:synthesis} says that these conditions together suffice in the model, and that each of the five, failing alone, can break the result. It does not say that any real clinic meets them.

"""

INS['counter'] = r"""\subsection{Forces that push the other way}\label{sec:counter}

The propositions are conditional, and each condition can fail. This subsection collects what the model says when four forces push against inclusiveness. We state only what follows from the propositions or from sources we have read, and we name which.

\paragraph{The premise fails: AI acquires tacit knowledge.} If AI can codify and reproduce conversion inputs, Regime~C applies. By Proposition~\ref{prop:regime}(iii), the holders' premium then stops rising with the fall in $p_c$ once $w(p_c)\ge k_t$, and it vanishes as the reproduction cost $k_t$ goes to zero; when the codified asset is cheaper to supply than holders' time, its owner earns the margin (part~(ii)). Condition~1 of Proposition~\ref{prop:synthesis} requires Regime~H, so the sufficiency result no longer applies. The model does not say that gains then become regressive, only that the guarantee is lost. Combined with \citet[\S12.4.3, pp.~310--311]{brynjolfsson2026hayek}, codifiability also restores the coordination argument for centralised ownership. Two other conditions change meaning. If AI supplies the conversion input, non-autonomy in our sense, a person being needed to supply it, is no longer implied by the premise; condition~3 then has to be assumed separately. And the apprenticeship externality of condition~5 rests on knowledge that only a novice can carry away; the model does not say what happens to it when that knowledge can be codified. The evidence on the premise runs both ways (Section~\ref{sec:evidence}, condition~1).

\paragraph{AI becomes autonomous.} By Proposition~\ref{prop:autonomy}(ii), with autonomy and $C(D)<z_A$, compute becomes scarce and earns $z_A-C(D)$. Holders earn $(1-z_A)z_j$, which does not depend on the density of the conversion layer and falls as $z_A$ rises. Investment in the conversion layer then raises the return to compute, not wages. In the joint economy, a rise in $z_A$ under autonomy gives holders a negative per-unit gain, and a negative gain multiplied by holdings that fall relative to income makes the factor-income part rise with income (Section~\ref{sec:synthesis}); Table~\ref{tab:necessity} reports such a construction. Where packaging is costly, $C(D)\ge z_A$, the case lies outside our proposition. In \citet[p.~35]{garicano2026comment}, opportunity creation then stalls and low-skill workers are displaced. Rules that keep a person in the loop, which \citet[p.~318]{brynjolfsson2026hayek} list, act against this force.

\paragraph{Upstream supply concentrates.} By Proposition~\ref{prop:market}, with lower pass-through $\rho$ less of any cost decline reaches households, and the progressivity condition is relaxed by less; a higher access fee $A$ raises the density threshold $n^*(A)$. Condition~4 then fails first in low-density catchments. A larger foreign share $f$ lowers the domestic welfare gain from a cost decline without changing the progressivity condition. \citet[pp.~106, 110--111]{athey2026competition} add general-equilibrium channels that our model lacks: double harm to unskilled workers, the shifting of extraction from a capped fee to the other, and capability loss that weakens a country's outside option.

\paragraph{Apprenticeship capacity shrinks.} By Corollary~\ref{cor:capacity}(iv), if each expert can supervise fewer than one apprentice, the share of each cohort holding $t$ falls geometrically whatever firms' demand, so no subsidy to demand can prevent the decline. With capacity exactly one, a single cohort's shortfall is permanent (part~(ii)). With capacity just above one, it is recovered slowly, and its cumulative cost exceeds its direct cost whenever $\chi a_L<a_H$ (part~(iii)). Anything that reduces how many novices each expert can supervise therefore acts on the part of condition~5 that a demand subsidy does not reach. The evidence on entry hiring \citep{brynjolfsson2025canaries,hosseini2025seniority} bears on demand, $a$; we found none on $\chi$.

\paragraph{The clinic (illustration).} In the clinic, the four forces would be: a platform that learns enough from the workers' notes to do without them; an assistant that contacts and treats patients by itself; a single supplier that raises its access fee when its usage fee is capped; and a clinic that stops training new workers until it has too few experienced ones to supervise trainees. In each case the clinic's gains would cease to be guaranteed in the model.

"""

PENDA_OLD = r"""This is the intermediated channel of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d): the AI output reaches the patient through a trained person who checks it."""
PENDA_NEW = r"""This corresponds to the intermediated channel of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d) only in part: the AI reviewed the clinician's work at each major decision point and flagged potential errors, and all final decisions were made by the clinicians (pp.~5, 7), so the study shows that a trained person working with an AI check makes fewer errors, not how often a person catches errors made by the AI."""

